WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.960
The fifth installment of the Pirates of the
Caribbean series is just as ridiculously fun

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.560
as any of the prior four chapters, and it's
just as chock full of Easter eggs, too.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:13.330
Here are some subtle -- and sometimes not
so subtle -- in-jokes from Pirates of the

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:17.250
Caribbean: Dead Men Tell No Tales that all
ye landlubbers will just love.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.930
Aye, but beware the spoilers.

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:20.930
What's in a name?

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:25.700
We didn't need Javier Bardem's Captain Salazar
to actually say the line…

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:29.020
"Dead men tell no tales."

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:32.689
… before the opening credits to get where
the title phrase came from.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:36.699
Previously, it was spoken in the third film,
At World's End, even if it was only said during

00:00:36.699 --> 00:00:39.260
a voiceover moment in a transition scene.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:43.060
Even earlier than that, the phrase appeared
in the original Pirates theme park ride.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:46.140
And remember this scene from Curse of the
Black Pearl?

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:52.540
"Dead men tell no tales."

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.900
Meanwhile, anyone who's been hungering for
some old school Johnny Depp swagger should

00:00:56.909 --> 00:01:01.319
be pleased with this film's extended glimpse
of the swashbuckler in his younger days.

00:01:01.319 --> 00:01:06.070
Thanks to Captain Salazar, we learn exactly
where, when, and how the Captain got his name

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:11.180
-- he defeated Salazar's squad by having them
steer into the Devil's Triangle to be obliterated,

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:15.690
and upon first taking command of his ship
and the compass, he looked like a sparrow.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.760
We also learn where he got all his odd accessories.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:23.310
After killing Salazar, who'd been on a mission
destroy every pirate ship in the sea before

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:28.140
being beaten by Sparrow, his new crew offered
him his signature beads and hat, among other

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:31.270
items of booty, as a tribute for his conquest.

00:01:31.270 --> 00:01:33.970
And thus his famed fashion sense was born.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:35.950
The Kraken doesn't speak

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:41.090
Henry Turner is the son of Will Turner, so
he'd definitely heard tale of the Kraken that

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:44.640
his father escaped in Pirates of the Caribbean:
Dead Man's Chest.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:49.750
So, when scientist-slash-skeptic Carina Smyth
makes an offhanded comment about his probable

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:54.440
belief in mermaids and the Kraken, he says,
"the Kraken doesn't speak."

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:55.440
And he would know.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:56.870
"I am a woman of science.

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.400
I choose not to believe in ghosts."

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.630
"Do you not see what's behind us?"

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.040
Interestingly enough, as it was described
to Will Turner, the Kraken has "the reeking

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.770
odor of a thousand rotting corpses."

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:11.900
And the attack mode of Captain Salazar's ship
is quite reminiscent of the way the Kraken

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:13.510
would down a vessel, too.

00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:14.510
And guess what?

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:20.329
It definitely smelled like rotting corpses
because it was staffed by undead sailors.

00:02:20.329 --> 00:02:21.980
Rock star pirates

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.500
It's no secret that Johnny Depp has a thing
for working with rock stars.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.769
In fact, he considers himself to be one.

00:02:27.769 --> 00:02:32.180
So, after borrowing some of Keith Richards'
style to inform Jack Sparrow's appearance

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:36.389
in the Pirates of the Caribbean franchise,
he helped the Rolling Stones guitarist snag

00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:41.049
an acting role in At World's End and On Stranger
Tides as his father, Captain Teague.

00:02:41.049 --> 00:02:44.189
"If enough people keep saying it, it must
be true."

00:02:44.189 --> 00:02:48.250
When Richards was unavailable to return to
the fifth film for another cameo, Depp turned

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:52.040
to another iconic musician and long-time pal,
Paul McCartney.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.580
Depp revealed that the filmmakers needed a
"pirate rocker" to sub in as his Uncle Jack,

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.650
and Depp thought of the Beatles legend first
thing, inviting him to appear in the pic via

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:01.650
text.

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.249
You just know their behind-the-scenes jam
sessions were lit.

00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:06.290
Johnny Depp's least favorite joke

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:11.110
Jack Sparrow's Uncle Jack offers him a joke
as he's about to be executed for his crimes

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:15.689
against the island: "A skeleton walks into
a bar, orders a mop and a beer."

00:03:15.689 --> 00:03:16.879
Simple enough, right?

00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:20.810
Well, Johnny Depp might not have gotten a
writing credit for Dead Men Tell No Tales,

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:24.900
but he certainly deserved one because that
was a quip he'd had a long history with.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.230
While he was filming Donnie Brasco, Al Pacino
would tell him the joke repeatedly, and he

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:30.590
never quite understood it.

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:34.389
He told The Express in 2010, "He thought it
was my problem.

00:03:34.389 --> 00:03:37.599
He inflicted it upon me and I could feel my
IQ points drop.

00:03:37.599 --> 00:03:39.599
I finally figured it out."

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:41.499
Obviously, he's come to appreciate the humor.

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:43.740
"Are all pirates this stupid?"

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:45.340
"Yes."

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:46.719
Nodding to the timeline

00:03:46.719 --> 00:03:51.849
When Captain Jack is en route to his execution,
he's given the choice between dying by hanging

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:54.680
or dying by the newfangled guillotine device.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:55.680
"Guillotine?

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:57.460
Sounds French.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:58.620
I love the French.

00:03:58.629 --> 00:04:00.599
Did you know that they invented mayonnaise?"

00:04:00.599 --> 00:04:04.780
While most of the Pirates films have only
loosely honored historical events and have

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:08.519
definitely fudged the timeline a bit, this
reference seems like a pointed effort to pin

00:04:08.519 --> 00:04:10.329
down a more precise date.

00:04:10.329 --> 00:04:16.450
The modern guillotine wasn't invented until
1789 and was first used in 1792, around the

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:17.750
time of the French Revolution.

00:04:17.750 --> 00:04:20.100
Although we've never seen it go quite like
this.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:25.440
Oh, and that very random mention of mayo may
have had some sly historical significance

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:26.440
as well.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:30.450
Legend has it that mayonnaise was first formulated
as part of a victory feast for the Duke de

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:34.040
Richelieu after he beat back the British at
Port Mahon that year.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:38.780
Captain Jack might not be known for his historical
humor -- and some historians believe it was

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:42.130
the Spanish who actually came up with the
condiment -- but the fact that he praises

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:47.590
mayo to a man of the British crown certainly
seems like a smart dig at the redcoats.

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:48.620
Thanks for watching!

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.190
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:53.320
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

